Review follow-up\n\nDecision\n- GpuMemorySnapshot.kind is the sole authority for forecast memory semantics. Forecast now consumes one complete snapshot instead of recomputing the kind from the host platform and reading total/usage separately.\n- Windows accepts total and usage as a pair only when both describe the same source. A zero usage value is valid and no longer triggers a second counter source. This prevents fabricated percentages on hybrid-GPU systems.\n- A missing system measurement plus Ollama GPU allocation intentionally emits VRAM with unknown numeric values (0/0); the UI shows the accelerator without claiming a measurement.\n- The Windows cfg spelling now consistently uses target_os = "windows".\n\nProof\n- TDD red: snapshot authority test initially failed to compile before profile_from_snapshot existed.\n- cargo test --lib gpu_vram: 14 passed.\n- cargo test --lib hardware_profile: 5 passed.\n- cargo test --lib ollama_manager::polling_tests: 8 passed.\n- cargo test --lib -- --test-threads=4: 3793 passed, 4 ignored.\n- Targeted Vitest: 2 files, 4 tests passed.\n- cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings, cargo fmt --check, npx tsc --noEmit, npm run lint, and git diff --check passed.\n- graphify update . completed.\n\nWindows boundary\nThe parser and source-coherence behavior execute in native unit tests on macOS. The PowerShell/WMI counter availability itself still requires a real Windows run; no native Windows success is claimed by this commit. Microsoft also documents inaccuracies in GPU process-memory counters, so source coherence is enforced even when a counter is available.
